  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 138 PM CST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\nSkies should continue to clear into this evening as the old upper \nlow makes slow northeast progress over the upper Mississippi Valley. \nThis system isn't quite done with the local area yet however as its \nsouthwest flank rotates through late tonight into early Thursday \nwith minor height falls occurring. Moisture and lift may be enough \nfor some very light rain or drizzle near the Nebraska state line. \nSouthern areas should see some increase in cloud too as a weakening \nupper trough shifts southeast out of the central Rockies and into \nthe Southern Plains. Precipitation with this still looks to stay to \nthe south. Another modest cold frontal passage with the \naforementioned wave keeps highs in the mid to upper 50s Thursday \nwith a slow modification of this nearly stagnant airmass into early \nnext week under split nondescript flow aloft with highs back around \n70 by Monday. Many locations that have not yet seen a fall freeze \ncould do so this weekend with light winds and clear skies \ndominating.",
